#!/usr/bin/env bash
# PreToolUse(Bash) ADVISORY — never blocks (always exit 0).
#
# Long deploys — scripts/update.sh, bootstrap.sh, host-setup.sh — must be DETACHED
# from the CC session. Neither way of holding them in a Bash call works:
#
#   FOREGROUND: the tool's HARD CEILING is 600000ms and a bare update.sh ran 1022s
#   here (MEASURED 2026-09-16), so no timeout value is enough.
#
#   run_in_background: NOT a fix, and this hook used to prescribe exactly that. It
#   has no 120s ceiling (MEASURED: a 400s background task completed clean, exit 0),
#   but it is bound to the SESSION's lifetime, and deploys launched that way were
#   killed mid-run on 2026-07-22 (server left DOWN during bootstrap) and
#   2026-09-16. The precise kill mechanism is UNRESOLVED; detachment removes the
#   coupling every remaining candidate runs through.
#
# WHY IT FIRES ON BACKGROUNDED DEPLOYS NOW. It used to return early when
# run_in_background was true, treating it as correct usage — so it was silent on
# exactly the input that kills deploys, which is why nothing ever contradicted the
# advice in practice. That early return is gone.
#
# HISTORY, because this hook is why a deploy died. PR #1221 shipped it and the
# skill's Timeout Policy at 23:48 on 2026-07-22, prescribing run_in_background from
# a CC memory written two days earlier. An incident that same day refuted the
# remedy; the correction was written on 07-23 into a DIFFERENT CC memory, and
# neither public surface was ever updated — so this hook taught the wrong remedy
# for 56 days, until a third deploy was killed by it on 2026-09-16.
#
# The durable lesson is narrower than it first looks, and getting it wrong is how
# this comment was itself rewritten: the remedy was never executed against the case
# it addressed. A remedy is only as good as the case it was actually run against.
#
# SCOPE. This hook decides WHETHER a deploy script is mentioned, not whether it has
# already been detached — that needs real parsing of both shell and systemd-run
# option grammar, and is deliberately out of scope here (issue #2088). So it also
# fires on a correctly-detached launch and on a read of one of these files. An
# advisory that over-fires costs a line; one that stays silent cost three deploys.
#
# Non-blocking by contract: EVERY path exits 0. It can never stop a command.
# See the genesis-development skill's Timeout Policy and CC memory
# `deploy_detach_not_background`.

# `|| X=""` on both: under an inherited SHELLOPTS=errexit (bash DOES import it, and
# settings.json spawns this as `bash <path>`), a failing command substitution in an
# assignment aborts the script before it reaches any of its own guarded code. jq
# exits non-zero on malformed input and is 127 when absent. MEASURED: without these,
# errexit + jq-absent exits 127, breaking the always-exit-0 contract.
IN=$(cat 2>/dev/null) || IN=""
CMD=$(printf '%s' "$IN" | jq -r '.tool_input.command // empty' 2>/dev/null) || CMD=""
[ -z "$CMD" ] && exit 0

# Only the long-deploy scripts.
case "$CMD" in
    *update.sh*|*bootstrap.sh*|*host-setup.sh*) : ;;
    *) exit 0 ;;
esac

# NO run_in_background exemption. It was here, treating backgrounding as correct
# usage, which made this guard inert in precisely the case it exists to prevent.
# Both surviving launch paths — foreground and backgrounded — are session-held, so
# both get the note.

# Surface the nudge via PreToolUse structured stdout (additionalContext) — exit 0
# + stderr is treated as "no objection" and is NOT shown to the model, which would
# make this guard invisible in the exact path it protects.
# Quoted delimiter: the text contains $HOME, $PATH and $(date ...) that must reach
# the reader LITERALLY. `|| true` because `read -d ''` returns non-zero at EOF even
# though it populates the variable, which would break the always-exit-0 contract
# under an inherited SHELLOPTS=errexit.
read -r -d '' ADVISORY <<'ADV' || true
ADVISORY: this mentions a long deploy (update.sh / bootstrap.sh / host-setup.sh). If you are LAUNCHING one, it must be DETACHED from this session. Neither way of holding it in a Bash call works.

FOREGROUND is impossible: the Bash tool's HARD CEILING is 600000ms (10 min), a larger 'timeout' value is not honoured, and an inner 'timeout N' does not extend it. MEASURED 2026-09-16: a bare update.sh ran 1022s. The SIGTERM at the ceiling is also not a silent truncation -- update.sh traps INT/TERM, and once the rollback trap is armed (just before the merge) an interrupt ROLLS BACK, unwinding a deploy that was going fine. Which phase a ten-minute kill lands in depends on the run, so treat it as "something will be undone", not as a clean stop.

run_in_background is NOT the fix, though this hook used to say so. It has no 120s ceiling (MEASURED: a 400s background task completed clean, exit 0), but it is bound to this SESSION's lifetime, and deploys launched that way were killed mid-run on 2026-07-22 (server left DOWN during bootstrap) and 2026-09-16.

Hand it to systemd instead, wrapping THE COMMAND YOU ACTUALLY RAN -- the example below deploys update.sh, but this hook also fires for bootstrap.sh and host-setup.sh, which are not interchangeable and take their own required arguments:

  systemd-run --user --collect --unit genesis-deploy-manual \
    --working-directory=$HOME/genesis --setenv=PATH="$PATH" \
    /bin/bash -c 'exec ./scripts/update.sh > ~/tmp/deploy-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M).log 2>&1'

Each flag is load-bearing. --unit is what leaves the session; 'systemd-run --user --scope' does NOT (a scope child keeps the caller's session id), and neither does setsid (it starts a new session but does not stop the caller waiting on it). --working-directory is required because systemd-run does not inherit this cwd. --setenv=PATH is required because a --user unit otherwise runs under systemd's default PATH, which omits ~/.local/bin.

Then VERIFY it took: 'systemctl --user is-active genesis-deploy-manual' must print 'active'. A failed launch is otherwise indistinguishable from a good one -- with a relative path and no --working-directory, bash exits instantly and --collect reaps the unit, leaving no trace. Check promptly (it also reads 'inactive' once the deploy has FINISHED), then poll the log. genesis-server going 'inactive' mid-run is EXPECTED.

This hook does not check whether you already detached, so if you did, ignore it. Rationale, measurements and incident history: the genesis-development skill's Timeout Policy.
ADV
# jq builds valid JSON (escapes the message). If jq somehow fails, fall back to
# stderr so the nudge is at least not lost, and still exit 0 (never block).
jq -n --arg c "$ADVISORY" '{hookSpecificOutput:{hookEventName:"PreToolUse",additionalContext:$c}}' 2>/dev/null \
    || printf '%s\n' "$ADVISORY" >&2
exit 0
